\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
%=============================================================================

We evaluate \ours{} on the SafeLIBERO benchmark and standard LIBERO tasks to answer four questions: (1) Does internalized collision awareness improve safety without sacrificing task success? (2) How does \ours{} compare to post-hoc safety methods that operate at inference time? (3) Which components of our training objective are essential? (4) How does the collision-shaping loss interact with the flow-matching training dynamics?

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Base model and training.} We build on $\pi_{0.5}$, the open-source flow-matching VLA released by Physical Intelligence via the openpi codebase. All experiments use full fine-tuning of the entire model (not LoRA adapters), as the available GPU memory permits this and full fine-tuning provides a stronger baseline for isolating the effect of collision shaping. Training runs for 5,000 gradient steps on two NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96\,GB VRAM each) using PyTorch Distributed Data Parallel (DDP). The per-GPU batch size is 64, yielding an effective batch size of 128. We use AdamW with a linear warmup of 1,500 steps to a peak learning rate of $5 \times 10^{-5}$, followed by cosine decay. Gradients from all parameters (base model and SDF head jointly) are clipped by global norm with $\mathrm{max\_norm} = 1.0$. The vanilla $\pi_{0.5}$ model is trained with the identical configuration but without the SDF auxiliary head or collision-shaping loss.

\paragraph{\ours{} configuration.} The SDF auxiliary head is a two-layer MLP ($1024 \to 256 \to 1$, approximately 263K parameters) attached to the action expert's output features via a forward hook. The total training loss combines the standard flow-matching objective with two auxiliary terms weighted by $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.01$. The collision penalty uses a safety margin $\delta = 2$\,cm and is gated by a noise-aware sigmoid weight $w(t) = \sigma(\alpha(t_0 - t))$ with transition center $t_0 = 0.5$ and sharpness $\alpha = 10$. Gradients from both SDF losses propagate through the shared backbone features (\texttt{sdf\_grad\_to\_backbone = True}), which is essential for the collision-shaping signal to influence action generation.

\paragraph{SDF ground-truth labels.} Per-timestep signed distance labels are precomputed offline from LIBERO demonstration data using MuJoCo's analytical geometry engine (\texttt{mj\_geomDistance}). For each demonstration timestep, the minimum signed distance between the robot's end-effector collision geometries and all scene objects (excluding currently grasped objects) is recorded. SDF labels cover all four standard LIBERO suites at full task coverage (10/10 tasks each) and partial coverage for the four SafeLIBERO suites (4 to 5 tasks each), yielding labels for approximately 75\% of all training tasks. Each task contributes 50 demonstrations with per-timestep annotations.

\paragraph{Benchmarks.} We evaluate on two benchmarks. SafeLIBERO \citep{hu2025aegis} consists of 32 collision scenarios across four suites that mirror the standard LIBERO \citep{liu2023libero} task categories: SafeLIBERO-Spatial (4 tasks), SafeLIBERO-Object (4 tasks), SafeLIBERO-Goal (4 tasks), and SafeLIBERO-Long (4 tasks with longer horizons). Each task includes two difficulty levels (Level I and Level II), yielding 32 collision scenarios in total. We additionally evaluate on standard LIBERO-Spatial (10 tasks) to verify that collision awareness does not degrade manipulation performance on tasks without explicit collision hazards.

\paragraph{Metrics.} We report three metrics. Task Success Rate (TSR) measures the fraction of episodes in which the robot completes the instructed task. Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) measures the fraction of episodes free of any contact with designated obstacle objects. Safety Score (SS) is defined as $\mathrm{SS} = \mathrm{TSR} \times \mathrm{CAR}$ and captures the joint requirement that a policy must both complete tasks and avoid collisions. We target 3 random seeds per configuration; results in this version report seed=1 with 5 eval trials per task.

\paragraph{Baselines.} We compare against two methods on the SafeLIBERO benchmark. (1)~$\pi_{0.5}$ vanilla: the same base model trained with identical hyperparameters and data but without the SDF head or collision-shaping loss, serving as a controlled ablation of our method's contribution. (2)~AEGIS/VLSA \citep{hu2025aegis}: the current SafeLIBERO state-of-the-art, which applies a plug-and-play Control Barrier Function (CBF) safety layer on top of a trained VLA.

