\subsection{Ablation Studies}
\label{sec:ablation}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

We conduct four ablation experiments to isolate the contribution of each design choice. All ablations use the same training setup as the full \ours{} model except for the specified modification. Results are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:ablation}.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Ablation study on SafeLIBERO (averaged across 4 suites). Each row modifies one component of the full \ours{} model.}
\label{tab:ablation}
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Variant & TSR & CAR & SS & $\Delta$SS vs.\ Full \\
\midrule
Full \ours{} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & --- \\
No collision loss ($\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0$) & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} \\
No backbone gradient & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} \\
Constant $w(t) = 1$ & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} \\
High collision weight ($\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.1$) & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} & \placeholder{P} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{No collision loss} ($\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0$, $\lambda_{\mathrm{SDF}} = 0.1$). This variant retains the SDF regression loss but removes the explicit collision penalty. The question is whether SDF prediction as a pure auxiliary task, without collision-specific shaping, is sufficient to improve safety. Accurate SDF prediction does not directly penalize collision-prone velocity vectors. The collision penalty provides a crucial directional signal: it specifically discourages flow-matching trajectories that enter the safety margin, whereas SDF regression alone only encourages distance estimation accuracy without preference for the sign of the distance.

\paragraph{No backbone gradient} (\texttt{sdf\_grad\_to\_backbone = False}). This variant detaches the SDF head's input features from the computation graph, so gradients from both $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}}$ and $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{col}}$ train only the 263K-parameter SDF head without modifying the action expert. This ablation tests the central claim that collision awareness must be internalized into the action generation weights, not merely predicted by an auxiliary module.

\paragraph{Constant weight} ($w(t) = 1$, no noise-aware gating). This variant applies the collision penalty uniformly across all noise levels in the flow-matching process, without the sigmoid gating function. At high noise levels ($t > 0.5$), the noisy action tokens $x_t$ bear little resemblance to the clean trajectory, and the backbone features primarily reflect noise rather than meaningful collision geometry. This ablation quantifies the importance of restricting the collision penalty to the low-noise regime where the model's representations carry reliable geometric information.

\paragraph{High collision weight} ($\lambda_{\mathrm{col}} = 0.1$, $10\times$ default). This variant increases the collision penalty weight by an order of magnitude while keeping all other hyperparameters fixed. An excessively strong collision penalty may cause the model to generate overly conservative trajectories that avoid obstacles but fail to complete tasks requiring proximity to scene objects.

